'''''Physalis angulata''''' is an erect herbaceous annual plant belonging to the nightshade family [[Solanaceae]]. Its leaves are dark green and roughly ovate, often with serrate margins. The flowers are five-sided and pale yellow; the yellow-orange fruits are borne inside a balloon-like [[Calyx (botany)|calyx]]. The exact native range is uncertain.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Physalis angulata'' (wild gooseberry) |department=Factsheet |website=keys.lucidcentral.org |url=https://keys.lucidcentral.org/keys/v3/eafrinet/weeds/key/weeds/Media/Html/Physalis_angulata_(Wild_Gooseberry).htm |access-date=2024-01-31 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite report |title=''Physalis angulata'' (cutleaf groundcherry) |last=Travlos |first=Ilias |date=2022-01-07 |doi=10.1079/cabicompendium.40711 |doi-access=free |lang=en }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=''Physalis angulata'' |series=Weeds |website=keyserver.lucidcentral.org |url=https://keyserver.lucidcentral.org/weeds/data/media/Html/physalis_angulata.htm |access-date=2024-01-31 }}</ref> The species may be naturally endemic to Australia<ref name=Oz>{{cite web |title=''Physalis'' |department=Factsheet |url=http://www.flora.sa.gov.au/efsa/lucid/Solanaceae/Solanaceae%20species/key/Australian%20Solanaceae%20species/Media/Html/Physalis.htm |via=flora.sa.gov.au |access-date=2024-01-31 }}</ref> or the Americas<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Physalis angulata'' L. |series=Plants of the World Online |website=Kew Science (powo.science.kew.org) |publisher=[[Kew Gardens]] |place=Richmond, London, UK |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:195334-2 |access-date=2024-01-31|language=en}}</ref> or the native range may encompass both the Americas and Australia.<ref>{{cite web |title=NT Flora |website=eflora.nt.gov.au |url=http://eflora.nt.gov.au/factsheet?id=24630 |access-date=2024-01-31 }}</ref><ref name=Oz/> It is now widely distributed and naturalized in tropical and subtropical regions worldwide.

The plant produces edible fruit that can be eaten raw, cooked, jammed, etc. However, all other parts of the plant are poisonous.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Physalis angulata'' (cut-leaved ground-cherry) |website=Go Botany (gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org) |url=https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/physalis/angulata |access-date=2021-06-13 }}</ref> Members of the [[Toba language (Argentina)|Toba]]-[[Pilagá language|Pilagá]] ethnic group of [[Gran Chaco]] traditionally eat the ripe fruits raw.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Arenas |first1=Pastor |last2=Kamienkowski |first2=Nicolás Martín |date=December 2013 |title=Ethnobotany of the genus ''Physalis'' L. (Solanaceae) in the South American Gran Chaco |journal=Candollea |volume=68 |issue=2 |pages=251–266 |doi=10.15553/c2012v682a9 |bibcode=2013Cando..68..251A |issn=0373-2967 |hdl=11336/8358 |hdl-access=free |url=https://bioone.org/journals/candollea/volume-68/issue-2/c2012v682a9/Ethnobotany-of-the-Genus-Physalis-L-Solanaceae-in-the-South/10.15553/c2012v682a9.full |via=bioone.org }}</ref> 
Unripe raw fruits, flowers, leaves, and stems of the plant contain [[Solanine#Solanine_poisoning|solanine]] and [[solanidine]] [[alkaloid]]s that may cause [[poison|poisoning]] if ingested by humans, [[cattle]], or horses.<ref name=ncsu>{{cite web |title=Physalis |department=Extension Gardener |year=2023 |publisher=North Carolina State University |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/physalis/ |access-date=23 February 2023 }}</ref><ref name=csu>{{cite web |title=Ground cherry, Chinese lantern |year=2022 |series=Guide to Poisonous Plants |publisher=Colorado State University |url=https://csuvth.colostate.edu/poisonous_plants/Plants/Details/93 |access-date=23 February 2023 }}</ref>

